the constant term","The initial $k+1$ terms centred at the same point","Only terms of degree higher than $k$","The entire infinite series for every smooth function"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cc25991817b10924e3de639e"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the $1$st order Taylor polynomial of $f$ at $x = a$?","options":["$P_1(x) = f'(a)(x-a)^2$","$P_1(x) = f(a)+\\dfrac{f''(a)}{2}(x-a)^2$","$P_1(x) = f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)$","$P_1(x) = f(x)$ for every smooth $f$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ed3e26a72ae2bb37d63158e"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the $3$rd order Taylor polynomial of $\\sin x$ around $x = 0$?","options":["$P_3(x) = x-\\dfrac{x^3}{3!}$","$P_3(x) = 1+x+\\dfrac{x^2}{2!}$","$P_3(x) = x+\\dfrac{x^3}{3!}$","$P_3(x) = \\sin x$ exactly for all $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c25d9c46d4c56b56f1d887b7"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which key property does $P_k$ share with $f$ at $x = a$?","options":["They have the same derivatives of all orders at every point.","They have the same derivatives up to the $k$-th order at $a$.","They are always equal on the whole domain.","They have no derivatives at $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e38b54a79b2c0456e8864265"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which distinction between Taylor polynomials and Taylor series is stated?","options":["A Taylor polynomial can be written only for real analytic functions, not for merely smooth ones.","A Taylor series always converges to any smooth function.","A Taylor polynomial never uses derivatives.","A Taylor polynomial uses finitely many derivatives; the full series equals $f$ only when $f$ is analytic."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4e7d77c6b67858b5fc8b56be"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $f(2) = 3$, $f'(2) = -1$, and $f''(2) = 4$. What is $P_2(x)$ at $a = 2$?","options":["$3-2(x-2)+4(x-2)^2$","$3-(x-2)+4(x-2)^2$","$3-(x-2)+2(x-2)^2$","$3+4(x-2)-\\dfrac{1}{2}(x-2)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"240b7049350cfaa2f812015a"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Given $P_1(x) = 2-x$ for $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$ around $x = 1$, what is $P_1(1.1)$?","options":["$1.1$","$2.1$","$0.1$","$0.9$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7dbda7de615a688ec55a77ca"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A student says: 'If I can write a Taylor polynomial, then the full Taylor series must converge to the function.' Which correction matches the definition?","options":["Taylor polynomials are unrelated to derivatives.","Taylor polynomials exist only for polynomials.","The full Taylor series never converges to any function.","A Taylor polynomial does not ensure the series converges to $f$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"76f8f6d1c5063d1f42dd0cd1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $P_k$ described as an asymptotic best fit rather than merely any polynomial of degree $k$?","options":["Because it has integer coefficients.","It matches $f$'s derivatives at $a$ through order $k$.","Because it equals $f$ at every point of the domain.","Because it ignores the value $f(a)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a5597eb6458e09e72fe58dc5"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which polynomial could be the $2$nd order Taylor polynomial at $a = 0$ for a function with $f(0) = 1$, $f'(0) = 2$, and $f''(0) = 6$?","options":["$1+2x+3x^2$","$1+6x+2x^2$","$2+x+6x^2$","$1+2x+6x^3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c955288c108485d8fd33c68d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A polynomial has the correct value and first derivative at $a$, but the second-order coefficient is $f''(a)$ instead of $f''(a)/2!$. What condition does it fail?","options":["It is no longer a valid polynomial.","It cannot be centred at $a$.","It generally fails to match $f''(a)$.","It has no constant term."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a651bfb75ad0dff4b28d22cf"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What data are used to build the $k$-th order Taylor polynomial at $a$?","options":["Only values of $f$ far from $a$","Derivatives of $f$ at $a$ up to order $k$","Only the integral of $f$","Only the zeros of $f$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b3d9ee1b04440ed6691d1676"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the coefficient of $(x-a)^k$ in $P_k(x)$?","options":["$f^{(k)}(x)$","$k!f(a)$","$\\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}$","$f'(a)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"740411b6d3952dc12e56a981"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the $k$-th order Taylor polynomial share with $f$ at $a$?","options":["Only the value at infinity","Only the zeros of $f$","Derivatives up to order $k$","No derivative information"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c20cc9db4d40a75a612585b8"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the first-order Taylor polynomial of $f$ at $a$?","options":["$f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)$","$f'(a)+f(a)(x-a)$","$f(a)(x-a)$","$f''(a)(x-a)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4d07a29c2920bdf9433cc07"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x)=x^2$ at $a=1$, what is $P_1(x)$?","options":["$x^2$","$1+2(x-1)$","$2+x^2$","$1+(x-1)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4beffc7edebe1abaabccc39f"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which is the third-order Taylor polynomial of $\\sin x$ at $0$?","options":["$x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}$","$1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2}$","$x+\\frac{x^3}{3!}$","$\\frac{x^2}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"690e888423f230e0a367db4c"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is a Taylor polynomial only a finite approximation rather than the full Taylor series?","options":["It contains infinitely many nonzero terms.","It does not use derivatives.","It can exist only for analytic functions.","It stops at degree $k$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"beaf435898c11b895161eeb1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(a)=2$, $f'(a)=-1$, and $f''(a)=6$, what is $P_2(x)$?","options":["$2-6(x-a)+(x-a)^2$","$6- (x-a)+2(x-a)^2$","$2-(x-a)+3(x-a)^2$","$2-(x-a)+6(x-a)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0ab358545f18887acff0547d"}]}}